  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 330 AM CDT Sun Jun 28 2026\n\n**** Dense Fog Early This Morning ****\n\nVery moist boundary layer conditions exist across central\nIllinois early this morning as evidenced by 07z/2am surface\ndewpoints in the upper 60s and lower 70s. While skies have\ngenerally remained overcast, there have been enough breaks in\nthe cloud cover to allow temperatures to cool to the dewpoint\nand areas of fog to develop. CAMs have a good handle on the\nsituation and have been consistently focusing the lowest visbys \nalong/east of the I-55 corridor. With the proper synoptic\npattern in place, a clear signal from the models, and area obs\nbeginning to show locally dense fog, opted to issue a Dense Fog\nAdvisory for the eastern half of the KILX CWA through 8am. \n\n\n**** A Few Thunderstorms Today ****\n\n07z/2am surface analysis shows a nearly stationary frontal\nboundary extending from northern Missouri to western Kentucky.\nScattered convection has formed immediately along the front and\nwill continue to skirt the KILX CWA to the S/SW through sunrise.\nAs upper heights begin to rise in response to a deep trough\ndigging west of the Rockies, the front will gradually get nudged\nnorthward today. As a result, a few showers/storms will develop\nas far north as a Galesburg...to Decatur...to Robinson line\nlater this morning before dissipating before midday. Areal\ncoverage will remain low (20% or less) and any storms that\noccur are not expected to be severe. Meanwhile further\nnorthwest, a cluster of strong to severe thunderstorms aided by\na 55-60kt 850mb jet streak over eastern South Dakota are\nexpected to track E/SE across southern Minnesota/northern Iowa\nthis morning. While this activity will eventually outrun the\nnocturnal jet, ample moist inflow from the south may help\nmaintain the convection in a much weakened state as it crosses\ninto southern Wisconsin/northern Illinois this afternoon. Most\nCAMs either show the cluster completely dissipating or keep it\nentirely north of the KILX CWA along/north of I-80. However,\nboth the HRRR and RRFS indicate it may dip a bit further\nsoutheastward toward the I-74 corridor. Given current upstream\nsatellite/radar trends and the consistent HRRR/RRFS solution,\nhave included 20-30 PoPs along/north of a Macomb to Bloomington\nline this afternoon. While the risk for severe weather will be\nlow with the decaying cluster, the latest Day 1 SPC Convective\nOutlook brushes Knox/Stark/Marshall counties with a Marginal\nRisk (level 1 of 5) for severe. If the cells manage to drop that\nfar south, think the primary risk will be gusty 40-50mph winds.\n\n\n**** First Summer Heatwave ****\n\nAs has been advertised for the last several days, the first\nprolonged heatwave of the summer is imminent. As the frontal\nboundary shifts northward, a hotter airmass will gradually\ntrickle into central Illinois today. The magnitude of the heat\nwill be mitigated by considerable cloud cover across the board \nthis morning and by convective debris along/north of I-74 \nthrough the afternoon. As a result, think highs will be held in \nthe 80s across much of the CWA...with readings at or slightly \nabove 90 confined to the W/SW. Decided to leave the current Heat\nAdvisory in place for locations along/southwest of a \nPeoria...to Decatur...to Robinson line this afternoon, although\nheat index values will likely remain below advisory criteria of\n105 degrees.\n\nOnce the front lifts into the Upper Midwest/Great Lakes and\nupper heights rise in earnest, temperatures will climb into the\nlower to middle 90s Monday through Thursday. With dewpoints in\nthe middle 70s, heat index values will peak at or above 105 \ndegrees each afternoon. Given increased confidence concerning \nthe strength/position of the upper ridge, have issued a \nprolonged Heat Advisory for Monday afternoon through Thursday \nevening.\n\nModels show a series of short-wave troughs gradually flattening\nand shifting the ridge eastward later in the week. With\nincreased cloud cover and rain chances, temperatures and\ncorresponding heat index readings will cool slightly for the\nupcoming holiday weekend.",
